## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical application of confidential computing to multi-tenant isolation in real-time ML workloads, beyond token-based controls.

## Key Concepts
- Confidential computing (TDX/Nitro/SEV)
- Per-tenant attested enclaves and key management
- Envelope encryption of in-flight data and outputs
- Attestation-based admission control at API gateway
- IR playbook with measurable success criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-spec showing attestation flow
function attestedInference(req, enclave) {
  if (!enclave.attest(req.tenant)) throw new Error('Untrusted tenant');
  const input = enclave.declassify(req.input, req.tenant);
  const out = enclave.infer(input);
  return enclave.envelope(out, req.tenant);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate per-tenant keys without service downtime?
- How would you detect and respond to a compromised enclave?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of CORS risks and practical mitigations in a fintech SPA context, focusing on per-origin controls, token usage, and incident response.

## Key Concepts
- CORS headers and policies
- Token-based auth and JWTs
- CI/CD security checks for config
- Incident response for cross-origin abuse

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example snippet for server CORS config (pseudo)
const allowedOrigins = ['https://app.example.com','https://admin.app.example.com'];
app.use((req,res,next)=>{ 
  const origin = req.headers.origin;
  if (allowedOrigins.includes(origin)) { res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', origin);
     res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Credentials','true');
     res.setHeader('Access-Control-Allow-Methods','GET,POST,OPTIONS');
  } else { res.status(403).send('origin not allowed'); }
  next();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate detection of misconfig in CI?
- How would you validate that CORS policies don’t block legitimate cross-origin traffic?

## Why This Is Asked
The question probes practical edge device security, supply chain controls, and IR readiness for OTA updates in a fleet setting.

## Key Concepts
- Hardware root of trust and device attestation
- Per-release key rotation and role separation
- SBOMs and artifact signing for OTA safety
- OTA gateway revocation and device rollback
- Incident response, containment, and forensics timelines

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation: verify OTA signature before flash
function verifyAndApply(image, publicKey) {
  const ok = verifySignature(image, publicKey);
  if (!ok) throw new Error('Invalid OTA signature');
  flash(image);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale key rotation across millions of devices?
- What logging and forensics data would you collect for IR?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply practical logging security to prevent token leakage in fintech contexts, a common attack surface.

## Key Concepts
- Log data leakage risks
- Redaction and sanitization
- Middleware for request/response logs
- CI gates for secrets in code
- Incident response and key rotation

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactLog(obj){
  const redacted = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj), (k,v)=>{
    if (/token|secret|api_key|Authorization/i.test(k)) return "[REDACTED]";
    return v;
  });
  return JSON.stringify(redacted);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the redaction effectively?
- How would you handle binary payloads or proprietary log formats?
- What logging libraries and CI tools would you choose and why?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of edge ID management, data encryption, and strict multitenant data boundaries across cloud services. It requires concrete controls rather than abstractions.

## Key Concepts
- Edge IAM with audience-bound JWTs and gateway introspection
- Envelope encryption and per-tenant key rotation (KMS)
- Snowflake RBAC + Row-Level Security and per-tenant warehouses
- CI/CD secret scanning and misconfiguration checks
- IR playbooks with traceability and forensics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Verify JWT audience at edge
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');
function verify(token, expectedAud) {
  try {
    const decoded = jwt.verify(token, KEY, { audience: expectedAud });
    return decoded;
  } catch (e) {
    throw new Error('Unauthorized');
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect token reuse across tenants?
- What metrics indicate successful policy enforcement?
